1
00:00:01,400 --> 00:00:05,179
Every week, new security fixes arrive for Amazon Linux.

2
00:00:05,579 --> 00:00:10,390
Servers that are already running need them too — without breaking the work they do.

3
00:00:10,790 --> 00:00:15,298
So how does AWS Factory keep running servers patched, safely?

4
00:00:16,298 --> 00:00:18,016
There are two roads.

5
00:00:18,416 --> 00:00:23,839
Road one: build fresh. The factory bakes the latest patches into a new image,

6
00:00:23,839 --> 00:00:26,093
so new servers start up to date.

7
00:00:26,493 --> 00:00:30,271
Road two: patch in place. For servers that keep running,

8
00:00:30,271 --> 00:00:34,723
AWS Systems Manager Patch Manager installs updates where they are.

9
00:00:35,123 --> 00:00:39,239
This film follows road two — but it starts in the factory.

10
00:00:40,239 --> 00:00:46,621
AWS Factory builds one hardened base image, and from it, three families.

11
00:00:47,021 --> 00:00:51,555
GitLab, which hosts our code. MongoDB, which holds our data.

12
00:00:51,555 --> 00:00:55,787
And the CloudOS workspace, which runs on an ECS cluster.

13
00:00:56,187 --> 00:01:00,923
Every server launched from these images carries a tag that names its family.

14
00:01:00,923 --> 00:01:03,790
In Patch Manager, that tag is its patch group.

15
00:01:04,190 --> 00:01:09,161
Three families, three patch groups — each with its own rules and its own time slot.

16
00:01:10,161 --> 00:01:15,105
The rules live in a patch baseline: which updates may be installed, and when.

17
00:01:15,505 --> 00:01:17,940
Security updates are approved at once.

18
00:01:18,340 --> 00:01:20,183
Other updates wait seven days.

19
00:01:20,183 --> 00:01:24,607
By then, the factory's image build has already tested the same packages.

20
00:01:25,007 --> 00:01:29,075
And the database and the container engine are never patched in place.

21
00:01:29,075 --> 00:01:31,257
An update there restarts the service,

22
00:01:31,257 --> 00:01:35,384
so they change only through a reviewed rolling upgrade or a new image.

23
00:01:36,384 --> 00:01:42,055
Every weekend night, Patch Manager scans each group and records what is missing.

24
00:01:42,455 --> 00:01:46,782
If nothing is missing, the group is compliant, and nothing else happens.

25
00:01:47,182 --> 00:01:51,136
If updates are missing, they install early the next morning,

26
00:01:51,136 --> 00:01:55,024
in that group's own quiet window — one group after another.

27
00:01:55,424 --> 00:01:57,928
Does every install need a reboot? No.

28
00:01:57,928 --> 00:02:01,379
Most updates apply while the service keeps running.

29
00:02:01,779 --> 00:02:03,806
When a new kernel does need one,

30
00:02:03,806 --> 00:02:07,416
the reboot waits for the group's planned downtime window.

31
00:02:07,416 --> 00:02:10,773
Then the server comes back, and the scan turns green.

32
00:02:11,773 --> 00:02:17,618
Here is the trap: a patch policy only installs on servers whose tag matches its group.

33
00:02:18,018 --> 00:02:22,548
A server with the wrong tag is still scanned — so it looks watched —

34
00:02:22,548 --> 00:02:24,546
but nothing is ever installed.

35
00:02:24,946 --> 00:02:27,254
Scanned is not patched.

36
00:02:28,254 --> 00:02:30,653
So coverage is checked, not assumed.

37
00:02:30,653 --> 00:02:35,452
The daily and weekly operations reports list each server's patch result,

38
00:02:35,452 --> 00:02:38,051
and flag any server with no patch data.

39
00:02:38,451 --> 00:02:42,550
Every rule change is tried on one server first, then rolled out to every group.

40
00:02:42,550 --> 00:02:44,989
And the owner approves every production change.

41
00:02:45,989 --> 00:02:50,093
Fresh images for new servers. Patch Manager for running ones.

42
00:02:50,093 --> 00:02:53,323
And a daily check that no server is left behind.
